iRiel Identity Continuity Specification
PRSL-AI / iRiel Ecosystem
Status: CANONICAL
Classification: Architectural Law
Scope: Identity, Context, Memory, Privacy, Retrieval,
Interaction, Security, Resonance, AI Providers
1. PURPOSE
This specification defines how the iRiel ecosystem
preserves identity continuity when a person’s identity
representation changes.
It establishes the separation between:
• Persistent Identity
• Identity Representation
• Historical Identity
• Context
• Memory
• Privacy
• Retrieval
• Interaction
• AI Providers
• Device Continuity
The purpose is to ensure that a person can evolve without
becoming a stranger to their own intelligence environment.
2. CANONICAL PRINCIPLE
Remember continuity.
Understand change.
Learn from evolution.
Present the current identity.
Respect the person.
3. FUNDAMENTAL DISTINCTION
Historical Identity is not Conversational Identity.
The iRiel may retain authorized historical information to
preserve continuity, integrity, security, and contextual
coherence.
The existence of historical information does not authorize
its disclosure during ordinary interaction.
Persistence is not disclosure.
Contextual knowledge is not conversational permission.
4. PERSISTENT IDENTITY
The canonical identity of a person is represented internally
by an opaque persistent identifier.
Example:
identity_id = iriel_identity_01…
The persistent identifier is the canonical reference for
continuity.
It must not contain unnecessary personally identifiable
information.
It must not be replaced merely because the person’s public
identity representation changes.
5. @IRIEL HANDLE
The @iriel handle is the user’s public identity key within
the iRiel ecosystem.
Example:
maria@iriel
The same person may later change the handle to:
joao@iriel
This represents:
maria@iriel → joao@iriel
and NOT:
maria@iriel → new person
The handle is mutable.
The persistent identity is not recreated by a handle change.
6. HANDLE CHANGE
A handle change must follow:
Change
→ Understand
→ Reconcile
→ Learn
→ Continue
It must never follow:
Forget
→ Replace
→ Start again
A handle transition is an identity continuity event.
It may update:
• Current identity representation
• Personalization
• Context
• Preferences
• Accessibility configuration
• Device relationships
• Agent relationships
• Authorized workflows
• Interaction behavior
It must not unnecessarily destroy existing continuity.
7. IDENTITY GRAPH
The system should conceptually represent identity
continuity as:
Persistent Identity
|
+– Historical representation
| |
| +– maria@iriel
|
+– Current representation
|
+– joao@iriel
Both representations belong to the same persistent identity.
The historical representation exists for continuity and
authorized identity operations.
The current representation governs ordinary interaction.
