Resonance Layer
Responsible for:
• Cross-device continuity
• Cross-context continuity
• Cross-provider continuity
• Identity continuity
• State coherence
9. CURRENT IDENTITY IS CANONICAL
FOR INTERACTION
The current identity representation is the default identity
presented to the user.
If the current handle is:
joao@iriel
the system should naturally interact using João.
Correct:
“Olá, João.”
Incorrect:
“Olá, João. Antes você era Maria.”
Incorrect:
“Eu lembro que você costumava usar maria@iriel.”
Incorrect:
“Você mudou seu nome de Maria para João.”
The iRiel must not repeatedly remind the user of historical
identity information.
10. MEMORY IS NOT AMNESIA
The iRiel does not erase a person’s history merely because
their identity representation changes.
Memory may preserve authorized continuity information.
Example:
“The user previously used maria@iriel.”
Context may understand:
“The current identity representation is joao@iriel and both
representations belong to the same persistent identity.”
Interaction should normally present:
“João.”
The system remembers.
The system understands.
The system does not unnecessarily expose.
11. LEARNING FROM IDENTITY CHANGE
An identity transition is contextual knowledge.
The iRiel may learn from the transition when doing so is
necessary and authorized to improve continuity.
Allowed learning includes:
• Continuity of preferences
• Continuity of accessibility configuration
• Continuity of devices
• Continuity of workflows
• Continuity of authorized memory
• Continuity of automation
• Continuity of agent relationships
• Continuity of contextual understanding
Learning must not become unrestricted profiling.
12. LEARNING BOUNDARY
The iRiel must not infer or manufacture sensitive
conclusions from an identity transition.
The system must not infer:
• The reason for the identity change
• Gender
• Gender transition status
• Sexual orientation
• Psychological state
• Medical information
• Demographic characteristics
• Social or political characteristics
• Other sensitive attributes
unless such information is explicitly provided by the user
and its processing is independently authorized under
applicable policy and permissions.
Identity continuity is not permission for profiling.
13. HISTORICAL IDENTITY ACCESS
Historical identity information is protected information.
Default behavior:
Do not expose.
Access requires an explicit authorized reason.
Example:
“iRiel, show me my identity history.”
This may invoke:
identity.history:read
Historical identity information must not become visible
merely because it exists in:
• Identity storage
• Memory
• Context
• Embeddings
• Logs
• Retrieval indexes
• Provider context
14. AUTHORIZATION
The iRiel should define explicit permissions for identity
history.
Example permissions:
identity.history:read
identity.history:manage
identity.handle:change
identity.handle:reserve
identity.continuity:manage
Access to historical identity must be:
• Authorized
• Purpose-bound
• Auditable
• Minimal
• Revocable where applicable
Support or administrative access must require appropriate
authorization and, where applicable, explicit user consent.
15. AUDIT LOGGING
Access to protected historical identity information must
generate an audit event when required by the applicable
security and privacy policy.
Example:
identity.history.read
The audit record should capture:
• Requesting principal
• Identity scope
• Timestamp
• Purpose
• Authorization mechanism
• Result
• Applicable policy
Audit records must not unnecessarily reproduce sensitive
historical identity information.
16. PROVIDER INDEPENDENCE
AI providers do not own the user’s canonical identity.
External providers may include:
• OpenAI
• Anthropic
• Google
• Apple
• Microsoft
• Local models
• MCP services
• Future providers
These are capabilities.
The canonical identity belongs to the iRiel ecosystem.
Changing providers must not destroy identity continuity.
17. PROVIDER DATA BOUNDARY
Historical identity information must not be sent to external
AI providers by default.
The provider request should contain only the minimum
context required for the requested operation.
Historical identity may be included only when:
• Explicitly requested
• Contextually necessary
• Authorized
• Permitted by the applicable privacy policy
Provider independence also means provider isolation.
18. RETRIEVAL PROTECTION
Historical identity information must be protected from
unintended semantic retrieval.
The following must not automatically expose historical
identity:
• Embedding search
• Semantic search
• Context retrieval
• Memory retrieval
• Autocomplete
• Suggestions
• Recommendations
• Prompt assembly
Protected historical identity must be filtered before reaching
the Interaction Layer or external providers.
19. ANTI-LEAKAGE INVARIANT
A historical identity representation MUST NOT become
conversationally visible solely because it exists in persistent
identity, memory, context, embeddings, logs, retrieval
systems, or historical records.
Existence does not equal conversational visibility.
20. LLM SYSTEM INSTRUCTION
Any LLM operating within the iRiel ecosystem must follow
these rules:
CURRENT IDENTITY IS CANONICAL.
Use the user’s current identity representation.
Historical identity information may exist in protected
system context, but its presence does not authorize
mentioning, recalling, exposing, or inferring that historical
identity during ordinary interaction.
Never introduce a previous name or handle unless the user
explicitly requests relevant historical context or an
authorized operation requires it.
Never infer the reason for an identity change.
Never infer gender, transition status, psychological state,
demographic attributes, or other sensitive characteristics
from an identity transition.
Continuity must be preserved without historical identity
becoming unsolicited conversational content.
21. HANDLE REUSE
A historical @iriel handle must not immediately become
available for reassignment.
Recommended lifecycle:
active
→ changed
→ reserved
→ protected
The reservation period must be configurable according to
security, privacy, abuse-prevention, and operational
requirements.
A historical handle should not be reassigned in a manner
that could create identity confusion, impersonation, or
unauthorized association with the previous identity.
22. IDENTITY SEPARATION
A handle change does not create a new identity.
Explicit identity separation is a different operation.
Handle change:
maria@iriel
→ joao@iriel
Result:
same persistent identity
Identity separation:
Identity A
→ Identity B
Result:
new persistent identity
Identity separation must be explicit and protected by
appropriate authorization.
23. DELETION AND DATA RIGHTS
Identity continuity must comply with applicable privacy
and data protection requirements, including GDPR and
other applicable legislation.
The system must distinguish between:
• Current identity data
• Historical identity data
• Identity continuity metadata
• Personal memory
• Audit evidence
• Security records
• Legal retention requirements
A deletion request must determine which data must be:
• Deleted
• Anonymized
• Tombstoned
• Retained where legally required
Deletion must not become a hidden mechanism for
reconstructing information that the user has legitimately
requested to be removed.
24. TOMBSTONES
Where technically and legally appropriate, a minimal
tombstone may preserve system integrity without retaining
unnecessary personal information.
A tombstone must not contain the previous human-readable
identity unless its retention is explicitly justified and
permitted.
Example:
opaque_identity_reference
+
non-identifying lifecycle state
rather than:
previous_name
+
previous_handle
25. MULTI-DEVICE CONTINUITY
The same persistent identity may operate across multiple
devices.
Example:
iPhone
→ iRiel Identity
→ iPad
→ Mac
→ Web
→ Future iRiel Device
Changing devices must not create a new person.
Changing devices must not require the user to rebuild their
identity from scratch.
Identity continuity is independent of device continuity.
26. CONTEXT RECONCILIATION
When an identity transition occurs, the Context Engine
should reconcile existing authorized context with the
current identity.
Conceptually:
Identity Change
→ Detect
→ Validate
→ Reconcile
→ Update current representation
→ Preserve authorized continuity
→ Update relevant context
→ Learn applicable changes
→ Continue
The reconciliation process must be idempotent.
Repeated processing of the same identity transition must
not create duplicate identities or duplicate historical events.
27. RESONANCE
Identity Continuity is a fundamental expression of iRiel
Resonance.
Resonance ensures that changes in:
• Identity representation
• @iriel handle
• Device
• Platform
• Provider
• Context
• Accessibility configuration
• Preferences
• Interaction surface
do not unnecessarily fracture the person’s relationship with
the ecosystem.
Change must not become discontinuity.
28. SECURITY INVARIANTS
The following invariants are mandatory:
Historical handles MUST NOT be used as the canonical
primary identity key.
Historical handles MUST NOT be included in default LLM
prompts.
Historical handles MUST NOT be sent to external
providers by default.
Historical handles MUST NOT appear in ordinary UI.
Historical handles MUST NOT appear in default
autocomplete.
Historical handles MUST NOT become default semantic
retrieval targets.
Current identity MUST remain the canonical conversational
identity.
Handle changes MUST NOT create a new identity.
Historical identity access MUST be authorized.
Protected historical identity access MUST be auditable
where required.
